  <knowledge>
    - The commands 'set skill', 'set goal', 'like', 'dislike' and 'reset preferences' can be used
  after a route is planned or started and will trigger dynamic route recalculation.
    - If the skier is already on the route when preferences change, the route's start time becomes
  the current time and the start point becomes the skier's current position.
    - When comparing two routes, each match with a positive preference is scored +1 and each match
  with a negative preference is scored -1.
    - The 'alternative' command prints 'avoided <id>' or 'no alternative found' if no alternative
  route exists.
    - The route with the higher sum of preference scores is chosen.
    - The 'show route' command prints the remaining route as a list of identifiers '<id> <id> ...
  <id>' where the first identifier is the next lift or slope.
    - The command 'quit' ends the program.
    - The 'load area' command prints the content of the loaded file verbatim.
    - Re-running 'load area' with a new valid area replaces the old area and aborts any running
  route.
    - On success the 'plan' command prints 'route planned'.
    - A ski area (Skigebiet) is a connected directed graph composed of lifts and slopes.
    - The 'list lifts' command outputs all lifts sorted by identifier ascending, one lift per line,
  in the format '<id> <liftType> <time> <time> <meter> <meter> [TRANSIT]'; 'TRANSIT' is appended for
   valley stations.
    - The 'list slopes' command outputs all slopes sorted by identifier ascending, one slope per
  line, in the format '<id> <difficulty> <surface> <meter> <meter>'.
    - The commands 'take' and 'alternative' may only be executed directly after the 'next' command.
    - The 'abort' command prints 'route aborted'.
    - The 'next' command prints the next identifier (<id>) or 'route finished!' if the route is
  finished.
    - If route scores are still tied, choose the route whose identifiers are lexicographically
  earlier.
  </knowledge>
